# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cad-sweep-blocks-bricscad-jobs-over-autocad — the sweep's hard failure gates jobs that never touch AutoCAD
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: ci
#+DEPENDS: cad-sweep-fails-jobs-for-zombie-pids
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

While fixing =cad-sweep-fails-jobs-for-zombie-pids= (clautolisp 2.2.110): among
the 22 jobs the sweep failed in native child pipeline 2885566207 was
=harvest:sysvars:bricscad:windows= — a *BricsCAD* job, stopped because three
=acad.exe= ids were in =alfe-created-cad.txt=.

* Why it is wrong

The sweep's hard failure exists for one reason, stated in the script: a wedged
AutoCAD owns the =AutoCAD.Application= COM registration, so the job behind it
would reach that instance and get =RPC_E_CALL_REJECTED= instead of a session.
That argument covers AutoCAD jobs only. A hung — let alone a DEAD — =acad.exe=
has no bearing on a BricsCAD session: different product, different COM
registration, different executable.

So a BricsCAD lane can be stopped by a condition that cannot affect it. The
zombie fix removes today's instance of this (the ids were dead, so nothing is
reported as a survivor any more), but the shape remains: one genuinely wedged
AutoCAD on the runner would again block every BricsCAD job.

* Directions

Sweeping everywhere is right — cleaning up is harmless and the runner is
shared. What must be scoped is the *hard failure*:

- =.cad-sweep-before= (.gitlab/native.yml) is shared by
  =.gate-windows-cad-hard= and =.gate-windows-cad-soft=, which every Windows
  CAD job extends, AutoCAD and BricsCAD alike.
- Give the script an intent-named knob — e.g. =CAD_SWEEP_NONBLOCKING=1= —
  read next to the existing =CAD_SWEEP_IGNORE_SURVIVORS= (which should stay
  what it is: the emergency override for a machine nobody can reach). Then
  either set it on the BricsCAD-only jobs, or add
  =.gate-windows-bricscad-{hard,soft}= templates that carry it, and switch
  those jobs' =extends=. The template route is the tidier one: the choice is
  then visible in one place per job rather than repeated as a variable.
- Jobs to classify: =*:bricscad:windows=, =probe:load-refusal:bricscad=,
  =verify:bricscad-*=, =*:accoreconsole:*= (accoreconsole IS AutoCAD, so it
  stays blocking), =vendor:probes:bricscad=. Read each job's script before
  deciding — a "bricscad" name that also drives AutoCAD must stay blocking.

* Acceptance criteria

- A wedged AutoCAD on the runner does not fail a BricsCAD-only job, and still
  fails an AutoCAD one.
- The sweep still RUNS (and still reports) in both cases: the cleanup is not
  what is being scoped, only the gate.
- A regression test on the CI configuration or the script text, so the
  distinction is not lost the next time the templates are refactored.

* Note

Filed rather than folded into the 2.2.110 fix: that change was about reading a
process's state correctly, this one is about which jobs a gate should govern,
and it needs the per-job reading above. AGENTS.md § /Change Discipline/ — do
not silently broaden scope.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.123

Every Windows CAD job was classified by READING ITS SCRIPT, not by its name
alone — which mattered: =verify:epure*= names neither vendor but both scripts
drive AutoCAD, and =vendor:probes:bricscad= passes =-Backend bricscad=, so the
name is right there.

*Twelve BricsCAD-only jobs* moved to new gates: =verify:bricscad-print=,
=verify:bricscad-hidden-ui=, =encoding:experiment:bricscad=,
=path-sysvars/getcname/pgp/optkw/pathname:probe:bricscad=,
=probe:load-refusal:bricscad=, =probe:suite:bricscad=,
=harvest:sysvars:bricscad= (all =:windows=) and =vendor:probes:bricscad=.

*Thirteen keep the blocking gate*: every =autocad= and =accoreconsole= job --
accoreconsole IS AutoCAD -- plus =verify:epure= and =verify:epure-api=, whose
scripts drive it.

=.gate-windows-bricscad-{hard,soft}= extend the existing CAD gates and add one
variable, =CAD_SWEEP_NONBLOCKING=1=, which =sweep-orphaned-cad.ps1= now honours:
the sweep still RUNS and still reports -- cleaning up after a killed job is right
whatever the vendor -- it simply does not fail a job that a wedged AutoCAD cannot
affect. It is kept DISTINCT from =CAD_SWEEP_IGNORE_SURVIVORS=, because the two
say different things: /this cannot affect me/ versus /I know, run anyway/.

*The `cad' tag is untouched*, so pjb's 2026-08-14 ruling stands: one global
mutex, no per-vendor pools, a BricsCAD job still cannot run beside an AutoCAD
one. The comment in native.yml says so explicitly, because a reader meeting
=.gate-windows-bricscad-*= would reasonably wonder.

*The classification is checked, not merely done*:
=scripts/check-cad-sweep-gating.py= (=make check-cad-sweep-gating=, wired into
=make check-release=) walks native.yml and fails when a job names AutoCAD on a
non-blocking gate, names BricsCAD only on a blocking one, or names NEITHER
vendor while sitting on a CAD gate — that last case forcing a deliberate
classification rather than a default. It also fails when it examined nothing at
all. Verified both ways: 25 jobs pass, and moving =harvest:sysvars:bricscad= back
to the blocking gate produces

: FAIL  harvest:sysvars:bricscad:windows drives BricsCAD only but sits on the
:       BLOCKING gate: a wedged acad.exe it never speaks to would fail it

Two sweep-script text tests extended (the knob, and that the non-blocking path
says WHY it continues); alfe suite 1234 checks, 0 failures; documented in the
alfe user manual, where the two variables are contrasted rather than listed.

** Acceptance criteria, one at a time

- [X] A wedged AutoCAD does not fail a BricsCAD-only job, and still fails an
      AutoCAD one — by construction of the two gates, and the check keeps every
      job on the right one.
- [X] The sweep still RUNS and reports in both cases: only the verdict is
      scoped.
- [X] A regression test on the CI configuration, so the distinction survives a
      refactor of the templates.
